window.docContents[17].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___14":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.14. Induced representations for $S_n$\tSection 5.14: Induced representations for S_n and definition of U_lambda","header":"Section 5.14: Induced representations for S_n and definition of U_lambda","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Section-5___14___-Induced-representations-for-S_n-and-definition-of-U_lambda/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___14"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations-of-k-and-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_/#Chapter2___Example2___3___14":{"contents":"Example 2.3.14. 1. A = k. Since representations of A are simply vector spaces, V = A is the only irreducible and the only indecomposable representation.\n\n2. A = k[x]. Since this algebra is commutative, the irreducible finite dimensional representations of A are its 1-dimensional representations. As we discussed above, they are defined by a single operator \\rho(x). In the 1-dimensional case, this is just a number from k. So all the irreducible finite dimensional representations of A are V_\\lambda = k, \\lambda \\in k, in which the action of A is defined by \\rho(x) = \\lambda. Clearly, these representations are pairwise nonisomorphic.\n\nThe classification of finite dimensional indecomposable representations of k[x] is more interesting. To obtain it, recall that any linear operator on a finite dimensional vector space V can be brought to Jordan normal form. More specifically, recall that the Jordan block J_{\\lambda,n} is the operator on k^n which in the standard basis is given by the formulas J_{\\lambda,n} e_i = \\lambda e_i + e_{i-1} for i > 1 and J_{\\lambda,n} e_1 = \\lambda e_1. Then for any linear operator B : V \\to V there exists a basis of V such that the matrix of B in this basis is a direct sum of Jordan blocks. This implies that all the indecomposable finite dimensional representations of A are V_{\\lambda,n} = k^n, \\lambda \\in k, with \\rho(x) = J_{\\lambda,n}. The fact that these representations are indecomposable and pairwise nonisomorphic follows from the Jordan normal form theorem (which in particular says that the Jordan normal form of an operator is unique up to permutation of blocks).\n\nThis example shows that an indecomposable representation of an algebra need not be irreducible.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.3. Representations\tIrreducible and indecomposable representations of k and k[x]","header":"Irreducible and indecomposable representations of k and k[x]","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Irreducible-and-indecomposable-representations-of-k-and-k_LSQ_x_RSQ_/#Chapter2___Example2___3___14"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Section-3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem-___-heading/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Section-3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem-___-heading":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem","header":"Section 3.8: The Krull-Schmidt theorem — heading","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/Section-3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem-___-heading/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem--Section-3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem-___-heading"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Unitary-representation/#Chapter4___Definition4___6___1":{"contents":"Definition 4.6.1. A unitary finite dimensional representation of a group G is a representation of G on a complex finite dimensional vector space V over \\mathbb{C} equipped with a G-invariant positive definite Hermitian formRecall that a sesquilinear form on a complex vector space V is an \\mathbb{R}-bilinear map (,) : V \\times V \\to \\mathbb{C} such that (zv, w) = (v, \\bar{z}w) = z(v, w) for z \\in \\mathbb{C}, and a sesquilinear form (,) is Hermitian if (v, w) = \\overline{(w, v)}. Recall also that a Hermitian form (,) is positive definite if (v, v) > 0 for all nonzero v \\in V. ( \\, , \\, ), i.e., such that \\rho_V(g) are unitary operators: (\\rho_V(g)v, \\rho_V(g)w) = (v, w).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.6. Unitary representations. Another proof of Maschke's theorem for complex representations\tUnitary representation","header":"Unitary representation","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___6___-Unitary-representations___-Another-proof-of-Maschke___s-theorem-for-complex-representations/Unitary-representation/#Chapter4___Definition4___6___1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Section-7___2___-Functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___2":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.2. Functors\tSection 7.2: Functors","header":"Section 7.2: Functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Section-7___2___-Functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___2"}});
